 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Incident Response Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is incident response automation?\", \"a\": \"Incident response automation is software that carries out the repeatable parts of handling a security or IT incident. It groups alerts, gathers context, pages the on-call engineer, and runs containment steps once a person approves them. Every action gets a timestamp, which gives the post-incident review a complete record.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you build automated incident response for a small team?\", \"a\": \"Start with one playbook your team already runs by hand, written as plain steps that name the trigger and who approves what. Describe it to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder'>AI automation builder</a>, connect your alert sources and chat tool, and replay a month of past alerts before it handles live ones.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are the benefits of automated incident response?\", \"a\": \"The first gain is time: lookups and the page start the moment an alert arrives, not when someone happens to notice it. The second is consistency, since every phishing report gets the same checks in the same order. The third is a record, with each step logged against its owner and time.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are the steps in an incident response workflow?\", \"a\": \"NIST's 2012 guide, SP 800-61 Revision 2, named four phases: preparation; detection and analysis; containment, eradication, and recovery; and post-incident activity. Revision 3 replaced it in April 2025 and ties response to the Cybersecurity Framework 2.0. The four phases still map an incident response workflow well, and the closing write-up suits <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/document-automation'>document automation</a>.\"}, {\"q\": \"Which parts of an incident response workflow are safe to automate?\", \"a\": \"Steps that only read data are the safest, such as looking up indicators or grouping duplicate alerts. Paging people and opening tickets are low risk too. Steps that change a system, like disabling an account or blocking a domain, are better held for a person's approval. Treat that hold like any approval workflow, with a named approver and a backup.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are the risks of automated incident response?\", \"a\": \"The biggest is a containment step firing on a false positive, such as locking out an executive who is traveling. Playbooks also go stale as tools and teams change. On platforms that bill per task, a flood of alerts can run up the bill. Apps built on Emergent have no task cap, and a burst of 500 alerts runs through the same playbook.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is the difference between automated incident response and security orchestration?\", \"a\": \"Security orchestration connects tools and passes data between them. Automation then runs the repeatable steps a person used to do by hand. SOAR platforms combine both for security teams. Automated incident response is the result either way: alerts handled by a playbook, whether that playbook runs on SOAR software or in an app your team builds.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are examples of automated incident response playbooks?\", \"a\": \"Malware containment isolates a laptop through the endpoint tool once an analyst approves. Leaked credential response forces a password reset for every account on the list. Certificate expiry checks open a ticket 30 days before a certificate lapses. Each one starts from a trigger and ends in an action someone can audit later.\"}, {\"q\": \"What metrics should you track in an incident response workflow?\", \"a\": \"Track mean time to acknowledge and mean time to resolve for each severity level, plus the share of alerts that turned out to be real incidents. Take the numbers from the incident record, not from memory. When the playbooks run on Emergent, every timestamp sits in a database you own, and the dashboard lives in the same app.\"}, {\"q\": \"What tools are used for automated incident response?\", \"a\": \"The tools fall into four layers. Monitoring services and SIEM tools raise alerts, a paging tool such as PagerDuty reaches people, and a chat tool hosts the incident channel. The fourth layer runs the steps: SOAR software or an app built for your team. Chores outside incidents, like on-call schedule swaps, are ordinary <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-automation-builder/task-automation'>task automation</a>.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
